@meta
id: 28-16
title: Scenario — four things wrong with this vimrc
chapter: 28
type: scenario
skills: vimrc-debug, vimrc-structure, vimrc-mappings, vimrc-filetype
par: 180
minutes: 15
vscode: a settings.json you inherited and nobody understands
open: .vimrc after/ftplugin/markdown.vim

@brief
A ticket, not an exercise.

    DEV-77  New starter's vim is misbehaving. Their config came from a
            colleague who left. Four separate complaints:

            1. TypeScript files get tab characters even though the
               config says expandtab.
            2. The leader mappings do not fire on comma. Backslash-w
               saves, which nobody asked for.
            3. Pressing the clear-highlight mapping sometimes does
               something strange after they add other mappings.
            4. Spell check is on in every file, including .ts, and
               lines wrap at 80 columns in code.

Two buffers: the vimrc, and their `after/ftplugin/markdown.vim`. There is
a `after/ftplugin/typescript.vim` in the tree as well — it is correct,
and changing it fails the check.

Work out each fault before you fix it. `:source %` makes the config live
in this session, and from there `:verbose set expandtab?`,
`:verbose nmap <leader>h` and `:scriptnames` will each hand you an
answer. Guessing is allowed, but the point of the exercise is the three
commands.

What the config should end up as: one `expandtab`, a leader that works,
mappings that cannot recurse, and nothing prose-specific left in the
global config — the spell and wrap settings belong in the markdown
ftplugin, as `setlocal`, with the `" --- Prose ---` header gone with
them.

@keys
| `:verbose set expandtab?` | the value, and the file and line that set it |
| `:verbose nmap <leader>h` | the same for a mapping |
| `:source %` | make the file you are editing live |
| `:19m16` | move line 19 below line 16 |
| `"a3dd` | cut three lines into register a |
| `:3,5s/^set/setlocal/` | rewrite a range |

@hints
Fault 1 is a line that contradicts an earlier one. `:verbose set
expandtab?` after sourcing names the line number outright. The last
setting of a global option wins, so the fix is a deletion, not a
reordering of the file.
--
Fault 2 is about *when* `<leader>` is expanded: at definition time. Any
mapping written above `let mapleader` was bound to backslash while the
file was being read, and stays that way. The `let` has to move to the top
of the section.
--
Fault 3 is `nmap` where it should be `nnoremap`. A recursive mapping
follows other mappings, so it changes meaning as the config grows. `nore`
is the default choice; the recursive form is for the rare case where you
want it.
--
Fault 4 is three global settings that should be local to one filetype.
Cut them into a register, delete the section header and its blank line,
and put them in the markdown buffer — then turn each `set` into
`setlocal`, because these files run per buffer.

@solution
A route that measures about 150 keystrokes, three of them diagnostic
commands rather than edits.

**Fault 1.** `:15d` — `set noexpandtab` sits at the bottom of the
Indentation section, added for somebody's Makefile. It is global, so it
beats the `expandtab` two lines above it for every file in the session.
Makefiles already get tabs from vim's own `ftplugin/make.vim`, so there
was never anything to fix.

**Fault 2.** `:19m16` moves `let mapleader = ","` to the top of the
Mappings section. Nothing else changes, and both mappings now answer to
comma. This is the one that people chase for hours, because the config
*reads* correctly — `<leader>w` is right there in the file — and
`:verbose nmap ,w` reports no mapping at all while `:nmap \w` shows it.

**Fault 3.** `/nmap ` then `cw` `nnoremap` `<Esc>`. With `nmap`, the
right-hand side is itself re-mapped: the day they add a mapping that
starts with `:`, or something involving `<CR>`, this one starts doing
their other mapping's job.

**Fault 4.** `22G` `"a3dd` cuts the three prose settings into register
a. `:20,21d` takes the now-empty header and the blank line above it.
`:w`, `:bn`, `G`, `"ap` puts them at the end of the markdown ftplugin,
and `:3,5s/^set/setlocal/` makes them local — a global `set` from an
ftplugin leaks into every other buffer you open afterwards, which is
exactly the bug you are fixing, moved to a new home. `:wqa`.

The generalisation is worth more than the four fixes. Three questions,
in this order, settle nearly every config problem: is it even mine
(`vim --clean`), what is loaded (`:scriptnames`), and who set this
(`:verbose set`). This config had four unrelated faults and never once
printed an error message, which is the normal case. Vim does what you
told it. The work is finding out what you told it.

And the reason a 500-line config from a gist is a bad idea is sitting in
front of you. Every line here is plausible. Every one of the four faults
is the kind of thing you write yourself at 6pm and cannot see at 9am the
next day. A config you grew one line at a time is one you can bisect in
minutes, because you remember adding every line and why.

@start .vimrc
" ~/.vimrc - one line at a time, when something annoys you.

set nocompatible
syntax on
filetype plugin indent on

" --- Looks ---
set number
set ruler

" --- Indentation ---
set autoindent
set expandtab
set shiftwidth=2
set noexpandtab

" --- Mappings ---
nnoremap <leader>w :w<CR>
nmap <leader>h :nohlsearch<CR>
let mapleader = ","

" --- Prose ---
set spell
set spelllang=en_gb
set textwidth=80

@expected .vimrc
" ~/.vimrc - one line at a time, when something annoys you.

set nocompatible
syntax on
filetype plugin indent on

" --- Looks ---
set number
set ruler

" --- Indentation ---
set autoindent
set expandtab
set shiftwidth=2

" --- Mappings ---
let mapleader = ","
nnoremap <leader>w :w<CR>
nnoremap <leader>h :nohlsearch<CR>

@start after/ftplugin/markdown.vim
" Markdown: prose, not code
setlocal linebreak

@expected after/ftplugin/markdown.vim
" Markdown: prose, not code
setlocal linebreak
setlocal spell
setlocal spelllang=en_gb
setlocal textwidth=80

@start after/ftplugin/typescript.vim
" TypeScript: Prettier's defaults, and a column to aim at
setlocal shiftwidth=2
setlocal colorcolumn=100
setlocal suffixesadd=.ts
